              '11': { type: 'exact', points: ['D'], guidance: 'Total area under Maxwell-Boltzmann = total number of particles. D.' },
              '12': { type: 'exact', points: ['C'], guidance: 'Catalyst lowers activation energy → higher proportion of collisions are successful. C.' },
              '13': { type: 'exact', points: ['D'], guidance: 'Ka = [H+][F-]/[HF] = (2.30×10⁻³)²/(7.70×10⁻³) = 5.29×10⁻⁶/7.70×10⁻³ = 6.87×10⁻⁴. D.' },
              '14': { type: 'exact', points: ['C'], guidance: 'N in N2O4: 2N + 4O; total charge 0; O is -2 each so 4×(-2)=-8; 2N=+8 so N=+4. In NO2 N is +4 also. In N2O4 N is +4 which is highest common. C.' },
              '15': { type: 'exact', points: ['B'], guidance: 'Vaporisation overcomes intermolecular forces (hydrogen bonds/VDW); covalent bonds within molecules remain intact. B.' },
              '16': { type: 'exact', points: ['B'], guidance: 'HCO3⁻ can donate a proton (H+) — Brønsted-Lowry acid. B.' },
              '17': { type: 'exact', points: ['B'], guidance: 'pH of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ NaOH = 14. To halve pH to 7 need to dilute 10cm³ to neutral: add 10dm³ water gives huge dilution near neutral. B.' },
              '18': { type: 'exact', points: ['D'], guidance: '[H+] = 10^-2.50 = 3.162×10⁻³; Ka = [H+]²/[HA] = (3.162×10⁻³)²/0.100 = 10⁻⁵ = 1.00×10⁻⁵. D.' },
              '19': { type: 'exact', points: ['B'], guidance: 'Sodium does NOT have the lowest melting point — argon (if present) would, but among Na–Cl sodium melts at 98°C, Si at 1414°C, S has low mp (~119°C), Cl is gas. Actually Na mp=98°C, Mg=650°C, Al=660°C, Si=1414°C, P=44°C, S=119°C, Cl=-101°C. So Na does NOT have the lowest mp. B is incorrect statement.' },
